[~accountid:712020:08485d64-da8c-4826-8ee4-512427d52ff5] thanks. I have now reviewed the ticket / Beehive / Admin in full - I note the following:

* *Reason for ID failure*: I appreciate that the reason for the suspension was failed ID verification, but do why know *_why_* the ID verification failed? Could we clarify this to the driver without revealing how our ID verification checks work? If so, we should do so, as this would be more likely to satisfy the driver. For example, I see from the ‘Comments’ section at the bottom of the driver’s profile that there is a note saying ‘FACES_DONT_MATCH’ - is this why? *Please confirm this with the stakeholders and include in the response as needed, as it may be helpful to clarify this*.
* *Appeal*: I see that the stakeholders flagged that drivers *_can_* appeal these suspensions decisions, but we have still not answered the question of why this driver has not yet been allowed to appeal. Ordinarily, drivers are given around 30 days to appeal - did this happen? If not, given the escalation on this, is this something that could be reconsidered now? Indeed, I see from internal comments that Dmytro Huminetskyi mentioned “_we can escalate to Verification tier team to handle this situation_” - *has this been done? Please push this forward with the verification team.* The core point is that the driver’s account has been restricted due to a failed verification - why can we not give them the opportunity to try again? It looks like this would resolve the issue and de-escalate the matter considerably.
* *‘Suspension’ language*: It note from [this|https://beehive.bolt.eu/customer_support/case/553571102] ticket that the driver can still actually access to their account - it is just that they cannot currently take trips ("_Please note that I still have access to my account through the Bolt Driver app. This means that the account has not been deleted, only access to orders has been restricted_”). As such, we should avoid saying that the account is ‘suspended’, as this is a bit misleading and weakens our own arguments from an Article 22 perspective. *Please keep this in mind for similar cases going forward*.
* *Privacy Notice*: When including links to the privacy notice, please ensure it is to the correct country.

Subject to the above, I have made some tweaks to the response - let me know if there is anything else that needs discussing on this 🙂 

